### Problem Restatement

We seek $p(t) = x_1 + x_2 t + \cdots + x_{2k+1} t^{2k}$ (degree $\leq 2k$) solving

$$\max \; \inf_{t \in \mathbb{R}} p(t) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad l_i \leq p(t_i) \leq u_i,\; i=1,\dots,m.$$

### Step 1 — Epigraph reformulation

Introduce a scalar $\gamma$ and rewrite

$$\max \;\gamma \quad \text{s.t.}\quad l_i \leq p(t_i)\leq u_i,\; i=1,\dots,m,\qquad p(t)\geq \gamma \;\;\forall\, t\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{$\star$}$$

The constraint $p(t)\geq \gamma\;\forall t$ is equivalent to the polynomial $q(t):=p(t)-\gamma$ being **nonnegative on all of** $\mathbb{R}$. The box constraints $l_i\leq p(t_i)\leq u_i$ are linear in $x$. So the only nontrivial part is encoding "$q(t)\geq 0$ on $\mathbb{R}$".

### Step 2 — Sum-of-squares characterization of global nonnegativity

**Theorem (SOS / Gram matrix, univariate case).** A univariate polynomial $q(t)$ of degree $\leq 2k$ satisfies $q(t)\geq 0$ for all $t\in\mathbb{R}$ **if and only if** there exists a symmetric positive semidefinite matrix $Q\in\mathbb{S}^{(k+1)\times(k+1)}_+$ such that

$$q(t) \;=\; s(t)^{\!\top} Q\, s(t),\qquad s(t):=(1,\,t,\,t^2,\,\dots,\,t^k)^{\!\top}. \tag{1}$$

*Proof of the theorem.* $(\Leftarrow)$ If $Q\succeq 0$ then $s(t)^{\top}Q\,s(t)\geq 0$ for every $t$, so $q\geq 0$. $(\Rightarrow)$ If $q\geq 0$ on $\mathbb{R}$, its real roots have even multiplicity and its non-real roots come in conjugate pairs, so $q$ factors over $\mathbb{R}$ as a product of monic quadratics with negative discriminant and squares of linear factors; each such factor is a square of a real polynomial, hence $q$ is a sum of squares of polynomials of degree $\leq k$. Writing $q(t)=\sum_\ell r_\ell(t)^2$ with $r_\ell(t)=\sum_j a_{\ell j}t^j$ and setting $Q=\sum_\ell a_\ell a_\ell^{\top}\succeq 0$ gives (1). $\square$

Expanding (1): $s(t)^{\top}Q\,s(t)=\sum_{d=0}^{2k}\bigl(\sum_{i+j=d}Q_{ij}\bigr)\,t^d$, so coefficient matching yields **linear** equality constraints linking $Q$ and the coefficients of $q=p-\gamma$.

### Step 3 — SDP formulation

Apply the theorem with $q(t)=p(t)-\gamma$. Let $c_d(Q):=\sum_{i+j=d}Q_{ij}$ for $d=0,\dots,2k$ (with $0\leq i,j\leq k$). Matching coefficients of $p(t)-\gamma = s(t)^{\top}Q\,s(t)$:

$$c_0(Q)=x_1-\gamma,\qquad c_d(Q)=x_{d+1}\;\;(d=1,\dots,2k). \tag{2}$$

Combining with the box constraints, the problem ($\star$) is equivalent to the **semidefinite program**

$$\boxed{\begin{array}{ll}
\text{maximize} & \gamma \\[2pt]
\text{subject to} & l_i \;\leq\; \displaystyle\sum_{j=1}^{2k+1} x_j\, t_i^{\,j-1} \;\leq\; u_i, \qquad i=1,\dots,m,\\[6pt]
& c_0(Q)=x_1-\gamma,\quad c_d(Q)=x_{d+1},\quad d=1,\dots,2k,\\[4pt]
& Q \succeq 0,\quad Q\in\mathbb{S}^{(k+1)\times(k+1)},\\[2pt]
& x\in\mathbb{R}^{2k+1},\;\gamma\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{array}} \tag{SDP}$$

### Step 4 — Verification of equivalence and well-posedness

**Equivalence.** Every feasible $(x,\gamma,Q)$ of (SDP) gives a polynomial $p$ with $p(t_i)\in[l_i,u_i]$ (linear constraints) and $p(t)-\gamma=s(t)^{\top}Q\,s(t)\geq 0$ (so $\inf_t p(t)\geq\gamma$). Conversely, any $p$ feasible for ($\star$) with $\inf_t p(t)\geq\gamma$ has $p(t)-\gamma\geq 0$ on $\mathbb{R}$, hence by the SOS theorem admits some $Q\succeq 0$ satisfying (2). So the feasible sets project identically onto $(x,\gamma)$, and the objectives coincide. $\checkmark$

**Convexity.** The objective is linear in $(x,\gamma,Q)$; all constraints are linear equalities/inequalities plus the convex cone constraint $Q\succeq 0$. Hence (SDP) is a convex SDP. $\checkmark$

**Leading-coefficient sign is automatic.** The coefficient of $t^{2k}$ in $s(t)^{\top}Q\,s(t)$ is $Q_{kk}$, so (2) forces $x_{2k+1}=Q_{kk}\geq 0$. Thus the SDP automatically enforces the condition needed for $\inf_t p(t)>-\infty$ (even-degree polynomial with nonnegative leading coefficient). $\checkmark$

**Attainment of the infimum.** When $x_{2k+1}=Q_{kk}>0$, $p(t)\to+\infty$ as $t\to\pm\infty$, so $\inf_t p(t)=\min_t p(t)$ is attained at a finite critical point. The boundary case $x_{2k+1}=0$ reduces to a lower even degree and is handled recursively by the same SOS/Gram construction with smaller $k$. $\checkmark$

**Existence of optimizers.** The feasible set in $x$-space is a polyhedron (finite intersection of slabs $\{x: l_i\leq a_i^{\top}x\leq u_i\}$). On this polyhedron, $\gamma\mapsto\inf_t p(t)$ is concave and upper-bounded (e.g. by $\min_i u_i$), so the supremum is finite; compactness of the level sets of the SDP (the PSD cone is closed, the linear constraints bound $\gamma$ above and the $x$-polyhedron bounds $x$) guarantees the optimum is attained whenever the problem is feasible. $\checkmark$

### Step 5 — Recovering the optimal polynomial

Solve (SDP) by any SDP solver (interior-point, first-order, etc.). The optimal solution $(x^\star,\gamma^\star,Q^\star)$ yields:

- the **optimal polynomial** $p^\star(t)=\sum_{j=1}^{2k+1}x^\star_j\,t^{j-1}$,
- the **optimal value** $\gamma^\star=\inf_t p^\star(t)$ (the greatest achievable minimum),
- a **certificate** $Q^\star\succeq 0$ proving global nonnegativity of $p^\star(t)-\gamma^\star$.

The size of the SDP is modest: one matrix variable of order $k+1$, $2k+1$ coefficient-matching equalities, and $2m$ linear box inequalities — polynomial in $k$ and $m$.

### Conclusion

The polynomial of degree $2k$ that maximizes $\inf_t p(t)$ subject to box constraints $l_i\leq p(t_i)\leq u_i$ is obtained as the $x$-component of the optimal solution of the semidefinite program (SDP) above, in which global nonnegativity of $p(t)-\gamma$ is encoded exactly via the sum-of-squares / Gram-matrix factorization $p(t)-\gamma=s(t)^{\top}Q\,s(t)$, $Q\succeq 0$. This is a convex SDP solvable in polynomial time, and its PSD factor $Q^\star$ provides a verifiable optimality certificate.

